{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261028T054348Z-05e19b74", "ts": "2026-10-28T05:46:07Z", "actor": {"worktree": "arrt", "plugin": "3.8.0-dev.2", "branch": "fix/hanging-badge-race", "build": "f355ab2d7e86"}, "body": {"base_commit": "9fc5d67e148e31dc4735b8588ac88731163911e9", "base_tree": "4f774ab51fd7033c800e25a05ed800935c02bbaa", "head_tree": "9b0bee3d24db02613eee9373126371803ed30eff", "head_commit": "2787031d942d713dc915691c62e87e8310b945c5", "dispatch_commit": "2787031d942d713dc915691c62e87e8310b945c5", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "rule-4 cumulative: working tree is clean, so chunk/final would dispatch an empty interval; branch is 1 commit(s) ahead of origin/develop and no cumulative record covers current HEAD \u2014 the committed bundle is cumulative's scope", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["arrt/tests/browser/test_hanging_a_theme.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/change-log.md", "arrt/tests/browser/test_hanging_a_theme.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/change-log.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "The fix stops one test reading stale paint, but ui.open still returns on stale paint for every same-document second open", "recommendation": "Learnings rule 'When you fix a defect, make inputs the fix does not know fail by name, and grep the commit for the same shape' applies here. The cause the change-log names sits in `UI.open` (arrt/tests/browser/conftest.py:292), not in this test. When only the fragment changes, `page.goto` makes a same-document navigation, and both of open's waits (`html[data-started]`, `#view *`) are already met by the previous screen. So the docstring's 'wait for the client's first paint to land' is false for any second open in a test. The new `section.wall` detached wait guards this one test, and only because the leftover screen is known to be Walls. An AST scan of arrt/tests/browser found 16 other tests that call `ui.open` more than once (test_asking x3, test_the_debug_view x3, test_the_sidebar x2, test_the_theme_screen x2, test_the_walls, test_getting, test_names_that_link, test_similar_artists_and_identity, test_the_sources_page, test_the_themes_index). The sharpest is `test_a_move_takes_effect_and_survives_a_reload` (test_the_theme_screen.py:112). Its second open goes to the same URL with the same fragment, so by inference (not measured) no reload happens: the half of the test its docstring calls 'the half that matters' reads the in-page table, not the catalogue. Fix the mechanism once. Either make `open` do a real document load when the shell is already up (goto, then `page.reload()` if the URL differs only by fragment), or have it wait for the previous `#view` content to detach or for the router's paint generation to advance. Then watch one of the siblings fail against the old helper. The test's own earlier comment ('the reload below is then free to overtake the POST', about line 79) also calls this second open a reload, which the new comment correctly says it is not. Reword it when the mechanism is fixed. Filing the sibling sweep to the backlog is an acceptable disposition. This commit's own change is correct.", "files": ["arrt/tests/browser/test_hanging_a_theme.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The race lives in `ui.open`, and the fix patches one caller of it", "recommendation": "Core rule 'When you fix a defect, make inputs the fix does not know fail by name, and grep the commit for the same shape' applies. The cause the new comment names is `ui.open` in arrt/tests/browser/conftest.py: on a fragment-only change `page.goto` does a same-document navigation, and its closing `wait_for_selector(\"#view *\")` is satisfied by the view still painted, though its docstring promises the client's first paint. 38 browser tests call `ui.open` more than once (awk count over arrt/tests/browser/*.py), so any whose next selector also matches the previous view has the same race. The new wait also ties this Theme test to Walls' markup (`section.wall`), so a Walls rename would make the wait pass on nothing. Better: fix it once in the helper, for example by clearing `#view` or waiting for a route marker the client sets per paint before `open` returns. Short of that, scope the badge read to the theme view instead of waiting on another screen's selector, and file the helper fix as a backlog item.", "files": ["arrt/tests/browser/test_hanging_a_theme.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "The older comment above the new one still calls the second `open` a reload", "recommendation": "Lines 81 and 84 say 'the reload below', and the new comment at line 88 says 'the page is not reloaded'. Prose only: no test or gate reads it. Change 'reload' to 'reopen' if the file is being edited anyway; otherwise no edit is wanted.", "files": ["arrt/tests/browser/test_hanging_a_theme.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings cross-check: the stale-paint mechanism lives in `ui.open`, and the fix patches one caller", "recommendation": "Class, not instance. Rule cited: 'When you fix a defect, make inputs the fix does not know fail by name, and grep the commit for the same shape'. The fault is in `ui.open` (arrt/tests/browser/conftest.py:292): its docstring says it waits 'for the client's first paint to land', but on a goto that changes only the fragment, `html[data-started]` and `#view *` are already there from the previous screen, so both waits pass at once. The new wait fixes one test. By a rough count, about 22 browser test functions call `ui.open` two or more times, and any of them that reads a selector both screens render can race the same way. To close the class, change `ui.open` itself (for example, wait for the view to repaint for the new route, or reload on a same-document navigation) rather than adding per-test waits. If that is not done in this bundle, file it. Nothing in this commit is wrong as written.", "files": ["arrt/tests/browser/test_hanging_a_theme.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog reconciliation: no open item is resolved by this bundle", "recommendation": "Scope none. `cache-query affecting` on the changed test returned 0 items (cache confirmed 4h old). A keyword scan of the open set found nothing about this flake. #57 (an MCP contract flake) and #390 (Themes UI) are unrelated.", "files": [".prawduct/change-log.md"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Records pass: covered .prawduct/change-log.md (the 2026-10-28 hanging-badge entry); nothing clears a bar", "recommendation": "Scope none. Checked against the code: Walls cards do render a `.badge` span through `stateBadge` in the card header (screens/walls.js, badges.js), and only the fragment changes on the second `open`, as conftest's `page.goto` shows. 'No product code changed' matches the diff, which has two files and touches no product code. The '0 failures in 60 runs' figure is a builder measurement nothing can re-check, but it is labelled as a run count, it ships nowhere, and it leads no one into a wrong action.", "files": [".prawduct/change-log.md"], "fid": "R-6"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 2, "note": 4}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 300, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `fix/hanging-badge-race`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunk": null, "base_reviewed": "9fc5d67e148e31dc4735b8588ac88731163911e9", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/change-log.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_grad